---
/** Sources and terms. Attribution is a condition of the licence, not a
 *  courtesy — SPEC §3 — and this page is where the condition is met. */
import Base from '@layouts/Base.astro';
import { getSchedule } from '@lib/data';

const schedule = getSchedule();
const { source } = schedule;
---

<Base title="Credits" description="Where the data comes from, and the terms it is used under.">
  <div class="wrap section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Sources and terms</p>
    <h1 class="headline" style="margin-top:.2em">Credits</h1>

    <div class="prose measure">
      <h2 class="title">The data</h2>
      <p>
        Everything on this site comes from
        <a href={source.url} rel="noopener">{source.name}</a>, which publishes NFL schedules,
        play-by-play and team information as GitHub release assets and refreshes them nightly
        during the season. It is used under <strong>{source.licence}</strong>, which requires
        attribution; this page is that attribution.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three datasets are in use: <code>schedules</code> for the {schedule.counts.games}-game
        schedule, <code>pbp</code> for the play-by-play behind every game page and replay, and
        <code>teams</code> for names, divisions and colours. nflverse in turn builds its
        play-by-play on the NFL's own published game feeds.
      </p>

      <h2 class="title">The club marks</h2>
      <p>
        The team logos — beside a club's name on its own page and on a game page,
        on the replay's scoreboard and in its end zones — are the clubs' own, and
        the shield in the masthead is the league's. A page loads only the marks of
        the clubs it is about. They are
        <strong>trademarks of the individual clubs</strong> and are not covered by the licence
        above — {source.name} publishes the addresses of these images as part of its data, but it
        does not own the marks and cannot license them. They are reproduced here for the one
        purpose of identifying which team is which.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site is unofficial and unaffiliated. It is not associated with the National Football
        League or with any club, and nothing on it is endorsed by them.
      </p>

      <h2 class="title">What is not here</h2>
      <p>
        There is no public player tracking for this season. The NFL's Next Gen Stats tracking —
        twenty-two players at 10 Hz — is released only for specific historical weeks through the
        Big Data Bowl, under competition terms that do not permit redistribution, and it does not
        exist for 2026 at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the replay shows the ball, the chains and the drive, and says so on its own face. It
        does not show routes, coverage or where anybody was standing, and nothing on this site is
        drawn as though it could.
      </p>

      <h2 class="title">How to read it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Kickoff times are US Eastern</strong>, as nflverse publishes them, and are not
          converted. Dates are rendered from the stored date and never parsed through a local
          timezone, which is how a schedule quietly loses a day.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Point spreads are home-relative.</strong> A spread of 3 means the home team
          favoured by three. They are only published about two weeks ahead of kickoff, so most of
          the season has none.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>EPA is a model</strong>, not an observation. It comes from nflverse, is
          shown as a number against each play in the log, and is never a certainty. Win
          probability is published alongside it and is deliberately not shown here.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A gap is shown as a gap.</strong> Where the ball was placed rather than carried —
          a penalty, a loose ball, a change of possession — the replay marks it and says why.
          Where the data has no answer, the page says that instead of guessing.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="title">The build</h2>
      <p>
        The site is static. A pipeline turns the nflverse CSVs into canonical JSON, a verifier
        checks that JSON against facts it did not produce — the published final scores, the
        league's own drive summaries, and the point values football allows — and the build fails
        if any of them disagree. The emitted data is committed alongside the code, so the site can
        be rebuilt from the repository alone.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Design and build inherit from a sibling project,
        <a href="https://github.com/DavidJCrawford/f1-analysis" rel="noopener">F1 Analysis</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose { --flow: 1rem; }
  .prose .title { margin-top: 2.2em; }
  .prose .title:first-child { margin-top: 1.4em; }
  .prose p { margin: 0.8em 0 0; color: var(--ks-text); }
  .prose ul { margin: 0.8em 0 0; padding-left: 20px; }
  .prose li { margin-top: 0.6em; }
  .prose code {
    font-family: var(--ks-mono), monospace;
    font-size: 0.85em; letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    background: var(--ks-paper-deep); padding: 1px 5px; border-radius: var(--ks-radius-sm);
  }
</style>
